const v18ReadA={id:"v18-read-a",kind:"passage",label:"Passage for Items 69-72",title:"Why the Moon Changes Shape",description:"Many people believe that the phases of the moon are caused by the Earth's shadow. It is a reasonable guess, but it is wrong.\n\nThe moon makes no light of its own. At every moment, the sun lights up half of it, the half facing the sun, while the other half is dark. What changes over the month is how much of that lit half we can see from Earth.\n\nThe moon circles the Earth about once every 29 and a half days, measured from one new moon to the next. At new moon, it lies roughly between the Earth and the sun, so its lit side faces away from us and we see almost nothing. As it moves along its orbit, a growing sliver of the lit side comes into view: a crescent, then a half moon, then a gibbous moon. At full moon, the Earth lies roughly between the sun and the moon, and we see the whole lit face. Then the sequence runs in reverse.\n\nThe Earth's shadow does fall on the moon sometimes, but that is a different event: a lunar eclipse, which can happen only at full moon.",alt:"Science passage correcting the belief that the Earth's shadow causes moon phases: half the moon is always lit, and its position decides how much we see; eclipses are separate."};
const v18ReadB={id:"v18-read-b",kind:"passage",label:"Babasahin para sa Aytem 73-76",title:"Ang Pinagmulan ng Pangalang 'Pilipinas'",description:"Nag-ugat ang pangalang 'Pilipinas' sa pangalan ng isang hari. Noong 1543, pinangalanan ng manlalayag na Espanyol na si Ruy López de Villalobos ang ilang pulo sa Kabisayaan na 'Las Islas Filipinas,' bilang parangal kay Prinsipe Felipe, na kalaunan ay naging Haring Felipe II ng Espanya.\n\nHindi agad sakop ng pangalang ito ang buong kapuluan. Ginamit muna ito para sa ilang pulo lamang, at unti-unting lumawak habang lumalaganap ang pamamahala ng Espanya. Sa paglipas ng mga siglo, naging pangalan ito ng buong bansa.\n\nBago dumating ang mga Espanyol, walang iisang pangalan ang buong kapuluan. May kani-kaniyang pangalan ang mga pamayanan at kaharian, at iba't ibang wika ang ginagamit ng mga ito.\n\nMay mga Pilipinong nagmungkahi sa iba't ibang panahon na palitan ang pangalan, dahil nagmula ito sa isang mananakop. Naniniwala naman ang iba na ang kahulugan ng pangalan ngayon ay hindi na ang hari, kundi ang mga mamamayang bumuo ng bansa. Patuloy ang pag-uusap na ito, at ipinapakita nito na may dalang kasaysayan ang pangalan ng isang bansa.",alt:"Tekstong pangkasaysayan tungkol sa pinagmulan ng pangalang Pilipinas noong 1543, sa unti-unting paglawak ng saklaw nito, at sa mga pananaw tungkol dito ngayon."};
const v18ReadC={id:"v18-read-c",kind:"passage",label:"Passage for Items 77-80",title:"When Antibiotics Stop Working",description:"Antibiotics are medicines that kill bacteria or stop them from multiplying. Since their wide use began in the 1940s, they have turned many once-deadly infections into routine illnesses. They have no effect on viruses, however, so they do not cure colds or influenza.\n\nEvery time antibiotics are used, they create pressure on bacteria. In any large population of bacteria, a few may carry changes that let them survive a particular drug. When the drug kills the others, these survivors are left to multiply, and over time the resistant type can become common. Bacteria can also pass resistance genes to one another, which spreads the problem further.\n\nMisuse speeds up this process. Taking antibiotics for a viral cold exposes the bacteria in the body to the drug without any benefit to the patient. Using them routinely to make healthy farm animals grow faster has the same effect on a much larger scale.\n\nThe result is that some infections, including certain forms of tuberculosis, have become hard to treat with the usual medicines. Health authorities therefore ask doctors to prescribe antibiotics only when they are needed, and patients to take them exactly as directed.",alt:"Health science passage on what antibiotics do, how resistant bacteria are selected and spread, how misuse speeds this up, and why careful use is urged."};
